WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.897
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.197 --> 00:00:08.735
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.035 --> 00:00:14.404
Unit twenty: Negotiation Skills. Today you will make offers with 'If you…, I'll…'.

4
00:00:14.704 --> 00:00:17.240
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.619
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.483 --> 00:00:23.566
Negotiate.

7
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.223
Negotiate means to discuss to reach an agreement.

8
00:00:27.817 --> 00:00:30.118
We negotiated the price at the market.

9
00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:33.021
Your turn. Say: Negotiate.

10
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:36.903
Negotiate.

11
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:38.686
Compromise.

12
00:00:39.683 --> 00:00:42.623
Compromise means an agreement where both sides give a little.

13
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:45.855
We made a compromise: one hour of games.

14
00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.207
Your turn. Say: Compromise.

15
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:53.195
Compromise.

16
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:54.893
Agreement.

17
00:00:55.883 --> 00:00:58.707
Agreement means a decision both sides accept.

18
00:00:59.317 --> 00:01:01.142
We finally reached an agreement.

19
00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.193
Your turn. Say: Agreement.

20
00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:07.953
Agreement.

21
00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:09.486
Offer.

22
00:01:10.483 --> 00:01:13.052
Offer means something you say you will give or do.

23
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.185
That is a fair offer.

24
00:01:15.783 --> 00:01:17.936
Your turn. Say: Offer.

25
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:21.708
Offer.

26
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:23.289
Listen.

27
00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:27.409
Listen means to pay attention to what someone says.

28
00:01:28.017 --> 00:01:29.540
Listen before you answer.

29
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.442
Your turn. Say: Listen.

30
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.069
Listen.

31
00:01:36.817 --> 00:01:37.760
Win-win.

32
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.551
Win-win means good for both sides.

33
00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.568
Sharing the room was a win-win solution.

34
00:01:45.183 --> 00:01:48.114
Your turn. Say: Win-win.

35
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:52.119
Win-win.

36
00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:53.826
Persuade.

37
00:01:54.817 --> 00:01:57.025
Persuade means to make someone agree.

38
00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.767
She persuaded her dad to let her go.

39
00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:03.349
Your turn. Say: Persuade.

40
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.224
Persuade.

41
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:09.008
Respect.

42
00:02:10.017 --> 00:02:12.957
Respect means treating others' views as important.

43
00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.387
Good negotiators show respect.

44
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:18.636
Your turn. Say: Respect.

45
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.559
Respect.

46
00:02:23.367 --> 00:02:26.948
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:30.433
Visal, how do you negotiate with your brother?

48
00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:35.376
I make offers. If you help me, I'll help you.

49
00:02:36.483 --> 00:02:37.821
Give me an example.

50
00:02:38.917 --> 00:02:41.415
If you wash the dishes, I'll clean our room.

51
00:02:42.517 --> 00:02:43.436
Does it work?

52
00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.806
Usually! It's a win-win compromise.

53
00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:50.513
Part three: grammar.

54
00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:54.592
Grammar focus: Conditional offers (If you, I'll).

55
00:02:54.892 --> 00:02:56.032
Look at the pattern.

56
00:02:56.717 --> 00:02:58.867
If you help me, I'll help you.

57
00:02:59.783 --> 00:03:02.096
If you lower the price, I'll buy two.

58
00:03:02.983 --> 00:03:05.273
If you lend me your bike, I'll fix the tyre.

59
00:03:06.233 --> 00:03:10.114
In an offer, use the present simple after 'if' and 'I'll' (I will) in the main part.

60
00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:14.280
Be careful! Don't say: If you help me, I help you.

61
00:03:16.233 --> 00:03:19.243
Say: If you help me, I'll help you.

62
00:03:19.543 --> 00:03:22.077
Use 'will' ('ll) for the offer in the main clause.

63
00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:25.484
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:27.591
Finding a Win-Win.

65
00:03:27.891 --> 00:03:30.390
To negotiate well, you need to listen first.

66
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.063
Then you can make a fair offer.

67
00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:36.879
If you show respect, the other side will trust you.

68
00:03:37.867 --> 00:03:40.482
A good compromise is a win-win for everyone.

69
00:03:41.467 --> 00:03:44.465
Persuading people is easier when the agreement is fair.

70
00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:49.754
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

71
00:03:50.617 --> 00:03:53.139
If you help me, I'blank help you.

72
00:03:53.439 --> 00:03:56.442
A, ll. B, d. C, m.

73
00:03:60.000 --> 00:04:01.791
The answer is A: ll.

74
00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.797
An agreement where both sides give a little is a blank.

75
00:04:06.097 --> 00:04:09.832
A, compromise. B, profit. C, offer.

76
00:04:13.367 --> 00:04:15.982
The answer is A: compromise.

77
00:04:17.117 --> 00:04:19.105
Good for both sides means blank.

78
00:04:19.405 --> 00:04:23.499
A, win-win. B, negotiate. C, persuade.

79
00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.905
The answer is A: win-win.

80
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.915
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

81
00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.529
When have you had to negotiate?

82
00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:45.910
Now listen to Visal.

83
00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:56.609
I negotiated with my parents about my phone time. I said, 'If you give me one more hour on weekends, I'll finish my homework first.' They agreed.

84
00:04:56.909 --> 00:04:58.699
Excellent answer, Visal!

85
00:04:59.583 --> 00:05:04.349
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and conditional offers (if you, i'll).

86
00:05:04.649 --> 00:05:08.961
Today's value is fairness. Look for solutions that are fair to everyone.

87
00:05:09.261 --> 00:05:11.809
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

88
00:05:12.109 --> 00:05:14.878
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

